-- src:4838 | interest of that person may be prejudicially affected by the
-- src:4839 | Commissioner’s decision;
@ref s 119(1)(i) (src:4834-4839)
GIVEN `the person interested has been duly summoned by the Commissioner to attend at the inquiry` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person makes default in attending` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 119(1)(i) the Commissioner may hear the claim and make the decision in the person's absence` MEANS
    IF `the person interested has been duly summoned by the Commissioner to attend at the inquiry` AND `the person makes default in attending`
    -- "even though the interest of that person may be prejudicially affected by the Commissioner's decision"
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if any person interested has been duly summoned by the Commissioner to attend at the inquiry and makes default in so doing" (an unmet "if", ruling (b), 2026-11-05): a party not duly summoned, or who did not default, is outside it
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:4841 | (j) the Commissioner must keep a case book, in which he or
-- src:4842 | she is to enter notes of the evidence taken and the decisions
-- src:4843 | arrived at in each case heard before him or her and must
-- src:4844 | authenticate them by attaching his or her signature thereto,
-- src:4845 | and the record in the case book is sufficient evidence of the
-- src:4846 | giving of any decision, or of the making of any order, and
-- src:4847 | of the terms thereof; and any person interested in a dispute,
@ref s 119(1)(j) (src:4841-4847)
GIVEN `the Commissioner has entered the notes of evidence and the decision in the case book and authenticated them by signature` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 119(1)(j) the record in the case book is sufficient evidence of the decision, order and terms` MEANS
    IF `the Commissioner has entered the notes of evidence and the decision in the case book and authenticated them by signature`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the record "in the case book" is sufficient evidence; the Act does not say what a record kept elsewhere is
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:4847 | of the terms thereof; and any person interested in a dispute,
-- src:4852 | decision or order, is entitled to a copy of the record upon
-- src:4853 | payment of the prescribed fee.
@ref s 119(1)(j) (src:4847-4853)
GIVEN `the person is interested in a dispute, decision or order` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the prescribed fee has been paid` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 119(1)(j) the person is entitled to a copy of the record` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the entitlement is of "any person interested in a dispute, decision or order"
        IF NOT `the person is interested in a dispute, decision or order` THEN NOTHING
        IF `the prescribed fee has been paid` THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "is entitled to a copy of the record upon payment of the prescribed fee" (marginal reading (a): a failed condition is a no)
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- src:4855 | (2) In hearing claims or conducting proceedings under this Part, the
-- src:4856 | Commissioner —
-- src:4858 | (a) is not bound to act in a formal manner or in accordance
-- src:4859 | with the Evidence Act 1893 but may inform himself or
-- src:4860 | herself on any matters in any manner that he or she thinks
-- src:4861 | just; and
@ref s 119(2)(a) (src:4855-4861)
GIVEN `the Commissioner is hearing claims or conducting proceedings under this Part` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 119(2)(a) the Commissioner is bound to act in a formal manner or in accordance with the Evidence Act 1893` MEANS
    IF `the Commissioner is hearing claims or conducting proceedings under this Part`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "is not bound to act in a formal manner or in accordance with the Evidence Act 1893"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:4855 | (2) In hearing claims or conducting proceedings under this Part, the
-- src:4856 | Commissioner —
-- src:4863 | (b) must act according to equity, good conscience and the
-- src:4864 | merits of the case without regard to technicalities.
@ref s 119(2)(b) (src:4855-4864)
GIVEN `the Commissioner is hearing claims or conducting proceedings under this Part` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 119(2)(b) the Commissioner must act according to equity, good conscience and the merits of the case without regard to technicalities` MEANS
    IF `the Commissioner is hearing claims or conducting proceedings under this Part`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the duty is "in hearing claims or conducting proceedings under this Part"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:4866 | (3) All proceedings before the Commissioner are to be held in
-- src:4867 | private.
@ref s 119(3) (src:4866-4867)
GIVEN `the proceeding is before the Commissioner` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 119(3) the proceeding is to be held in private` MEANS
    IF `the proceeding is before the Commissioner`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the rule is for "all proceedings before the Commissioner"
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 120 Representation before Commissioner`

-- src:4873 | 120. In proceedings before the Commissioner, a party —
-- src:4875 | (a) being an employee and a member of a trade union that has
-- src:4876 | been given recognition under Part 3 of the Industrial
-- src:4877 | Relations Act 1960 by the employer of the employee, may
-- src:4878 | be represented by an officer of the trade union; and
-- src:4880 | (b) being an employer may be represented by one of the
-- src:4881 | employer’s employees,
-- src:4883 | but must not be represented by an advocate or solicitor or a paid
-- src:4884 | agent.
@ref s 120 (src:4873-4884)
GIVEN `the party is an employee and a member of a trade union that has been given recognition under Part 3 of the Industrial Relations Act 1960 by the employer of the employee` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the party is an employer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is an officer of that trade union` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is one of the employer's employees` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is an advocate or solicitor or a paid agent` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 120 the party may be represented by the person` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "but must not be represented by an advocate or solicitor or a paid agent"
        IF `the person is an advocate or solicitor or a paid agent` THEN JUST FALSE
        IF `the party is an employee and a member of a trade union that has been given recognition under Part 3 of the Industrial Relations Act 1960 by the employer of the employee` AND `the person is an officer of that trade union` THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `the party is an employer` AND `the person is one of the employer's employees` THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 120 names who a party may be represented by, and does not say a party may not be represented by anyone else
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

§§ `s 121 Joining of claims`

-- src:4890 | 121.—(1) In proceedings under this Part where it appears to the
-- src:4891 | Commissioner that there are more employees or subcontractors for
-- src:4892 | labour than one having a common claim or similar claims against the
-- src:4893 | same employer or person liable, it is not necessary for each of them to
-- src:4894 | make a separate claim under this Part, but the Commissioner may
-- src:4895 | permit one or more of them to lodge a memorandum or make a claim
-- src:4896 | and to attend and act on behalf of and generally to represent the
-- src:4897 | others, and the Commissioner may proceed to adjudicate on the
-- src:4902 | several or joint claim of each and every such employee or
-- src:4903 | subcontractor for labour.
@ref s 121(1) (src:4890-4903)
GIVEN `it appears to the Commissioner that more than one employee or subcontractor for labour has a common claim or similar claims against the same employer or person liable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 121(1) it is necessary for each of the employees or subcontractors for labour to make a separate claim` MEANS
    IF `it appears to the Commissioner that more than one employee or subcontractor for labour has a common claim or similar claims against the same employer or person liable`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "it is not necessary for each of them to make a separate claim under this Part"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection speaks of the case where it so appears to the Commissioner
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:4890 | 121.—(1) In proceedings under this Part where it appears to the
-- src:4891 | Commissioner that there are more employees or subcontractors for
-- src:4892 | labour than one having a common claim or similar claims against the
-- src:4893 | same employer or person liable, it is not necessary for each of them to
-- src:4894 | make a separate claim under this Part, but the Commissioner may
-- src:4895 | permit one or more of them to lodge a memorandum or make a claim
-- src:4896 | and to attend and act on behalf of and generally to represent the
-- src:4897 | others, and the Commissioner may proceed to adjudicate on the
-- src:4902 | several or joint claim of each and every such employee or
-- src:4903 | subcontractor for labour.
@ref s 121(1) (src:4890-4903)
GIVEN `it appears to the Commissioner that more than one employee or subcontractor for labour has a common claim or similar claims against the same employer or person liable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 121(1) the Commissioner may permit one or more of them to lodge a memorandum or make a claim and to represent the others` MEANS
    IF `it appears to the Commissioner that more than one employee or subcontractor for labour has a common claim or similar claims against the same employer or person liable`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "where it appears to the Commissioner that there are more employees or subcontractors for labour than one having a common claim or similar claims" (ruling (b), 2026-11-05): with one claimant only the permission is not given
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:4905 | (2) Where the Commissioner is of the opinion that the interest of the
-- src:4906 | employer or person liable is or is likely to be prejudiced by the
-- src:4907 | non‑attendance of any employee or subcontractor for labour, the
-- src:4908 | Commissioner must require the personal attendance of the employee
-- src:4909 | or subcontractor for labour.
@ref s 121(2) (src:4905-4909)
GIVEN `the Commissioner is of the opinion that the interest of the employer or person liable is or is likely to be prejudiced by the non-attendance of an employee or subcontractor for labour` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 121(2) the Commissioner must require the personal attendance of the employee or subcontractor for labour` MEANS
    IF `the Commissioner is of the opinion that the interest of the employer or person liable is or is likely to be prejudiced by the non-attendance of an employee or subcontractor for labour`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where the Commissioner is of the opinion that the interest of the employer or person liable is or is likely to be prejudiced by the non-attendance" (ruling (b), 2026-11-05): without that opinion the duty does not arise
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 122 Jurisdiction of courts not affected; s 123 No division of claims`

-- src:4913 | 122. Nothing in this Part limits or affects the jurisdiction of any
-- src:4914 | court.
@ref s 122 (src:4913-4914)
GIVEN `the jurisdiction in question is the jurisdiction of a court` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 122 this Part limits or affects the jurisdiction of the court` MEANS
    IF `the jurisdiction in question is the jurisdiction of a court`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Nothing in this Part limits or affects the jurisdiction of any court"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:4918 | 123. No claim is to be divided and pursued in separate proceedings
-- src:4919 | before the Commissioner for the sole purpose of bringing the sum
-- src:4920 | claimed in each of such proceedings within the jurisdiction of this
-- src:4921 | Act.
@ref s 123 (src:4918-4921)
GIVEN `the sole purpose is to bring the sum claimed in each proceeding within the jurisdiction of this Act` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 123 the claim may be divided and pursued in separate proceedings` MEANS
    IF `the sole purpose is to bring the sum claimed in each proceeding within the jurisdiction of this Act`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "No claim is to be divided and pursued in separate proceedings before the Commissioner for the sole purpose of bringing the sum claimed in each of such proceedings within the jurisdiction of this Act"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the rule forbids dividing a claim for that sole purpose and says nothing of a division for another purpose
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 124 Investigations of complaints and offences`

-- The core's list of officials and bodies has no Parliamentary Secretary and no Permanent Secretary, so the
-- list of s 124(1)(a) is declared here (NEEDS-CORE.md).
DECLARE `s 124(1)(a) Kind of person` IS ONE OF
    `s 124(1)(a) the Minister`
    `s 124(1)(a) the Parliamentary Secretary to the Minister`
    `s 124(1)(a) the Permanent Secretary to the Ministry of Manpower`
    `s 124(1)(a) the Commissioner`
    `s 124(1)(a) an inspecting officer`
    `s 124(1)(a) some other person`

-- src:4929 | (a) the Minister, the Parliamentary Secretary to the Minister,
-- src:4930 | the Permanent Secretary to the Ministry of Manpower, the
-- src:4931 | Commissioner or an inspecting officer (each called in this
-- src:4932 | section a relevant authority) has reasonable grounds for
-- src:4933 | believing that an offence under this Act has been
-- src:4934 | committed, or wishes to inquire into any matter for
@ref s 124(1)(a) (src:4929-4934)
GIVEN person IS A `s 124(1)(a) Kind of person`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 124(1)(a) "relevant authority" the person is one` MEANS
    CONSIDER person
    WHEN `s 124(1)(a) the Minister` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 124(1)(a) the Parliamentary Secretary to the Minister` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 124(1)(a) the Permanent Secretary to the Ministry of Manpower` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 124(1)(a) the Commissioner` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 124(1)(a) an inspecting officer` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): "(each called in this section a relevant authority)" labels the five it names and does not say that no other person is one (fork F9, marginal reading (b): the other reading takes the label as a closed definition and answers JUST FALSE)
    WHEN `s 124(1)(a) some other person` THEN NOTHING

-- src:4929 | (a) the Minister, the Parliamentary Secretary to the Minister,
-- src:4930 | the Permanent Secretary to the Ministry of Manpower, the
-- src:4931 | Commissioner or an inspecting officer (each called in this
-- src:4932 | section a relevant authority) has reasonable grounds for
-- src:4933 | believing that an offence under this Act has been
-- src:4934 | committed, or wishes to inquire into any matter for
-- src:4935 | which provision is made by this Act or any dispute as to
-- src:4936 | such matter or the death of an employee, or any matter
-- src:4937 | connected with hospital and medical facilities, quarters,
-- src:4938 | sanitation, inspections or the keeping of registers and other
-- src:4939 | documents; or
-- src:4941 | (b) whenever any person complains to a relevant authority of
-- src:4942 | any breach of any provision of this Act,
-- src:4944 | the relevant authority may summon any other person who the relevant
-- src:4945 | authority has reason to believe can give information regarding the
-- src:4950 | subject matter of the inquiry or complaint, and the person so
-- src:4951 | summoned is legally bound to attend at the time and place specified in
-- src:4952 | the summons to furnish information or documents, produce any
-- src:4953 | article or give statements, and to answer truthfully all questions which
-- src:4954 | the relevant authority may put to the person so summoned.
@ref s 124(1) (src:4929-4954)
GIVEN `the answer of s 124(1)(a) "relevant authority"` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the relevant authority has reasonable grounds for believing that an offence under this Act has been committed, or wishes to inquire into a matter for which this Act makes provision` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a person has complained to the relevant authority of a breach of a provision of this Act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the relevant authority has reason to believe that the other person can give information regarding the subject matter of the inquiry or complaint` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 124(1) the relevant authority may summon the person` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Whenever (a) ... has reasonable grounds for believing that an offence under this Act has been committed, or wishes to inquire into any matter ...; or (b) ... any person complains to a relevant authority" (ruling (b), 2026-11-05: "whenever" counts as "where"): with neither limb met the power is not given
        IF NOT (`the relevant authority has reasonable grounds for believing that an offence under this Act has been committed, or wishes to inquire into a matter for which this Act makes provision` OR `a person has complained to the relevant authority of a breach of a provision of this Act`) THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): whether the person is a relevant authority is the answer of s 124(1)(a) (fork F9)
        IF NOT (`the answer of s 124(1)(a) "relevant authority"` EQUALS JUST TRUE) THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power is to summon "any other person who the relevant authority has reason to believe can give information regarding the subject matter of the inquiry or complaint"; the Act does not say what the relevant authority may do about another person (ruling (c), 2026-11-05)
        IF NOT `the relevant authority has reason to believe that the other person can give information regarding the subject matter of the inquiry or complaint` THEN NOTHING
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:4944 | the relevant authority may summon any other person who the relevant
-- src:4945 | authority has reason to believe can give information regarding the
-- src:4950 | subject matter of the inquiry or complaint, and the person so
-- src:4951 | summoned is legally bound to attend at the time and place specified in
-- src:4952 | the summons to furnish information or documents, produce any
-- src:4953 | article or give statements, and to answer truthfully all questions which
-- src:4954 | the relevant authority may put to the person so summoned.
@ref s 124(1) (src:4944-4954)
GIVEN `the person has been summoned by a relevant authority under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 124(1) the person summoned is legally bound to attend and to furnish information or documents, produce any article or give statements` MEANS
    IF `the person has been summoned by a relevant authority under subsection (1)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the duty is of "the person so summoned"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:4944 | the relevant authority may summon any other person who the relevant
-- src:4945 | authority has reason to believe can give information regarding the
-- src:4950 | subject matter of the inquiry or complaint, and the person so
-- src:4951 | summoned is legally bound to attend at the time and place specified in
-- src:4952 | the summons to furnish information or documents, produce any
-- src:4953 | article or give statements, and to answer truthfully all questions which
-- src:4954 | the relevant authority may put to the person so summoned.
@ref s 124(1) (src:4944-4954)
GIVEN `the person has been summoned by a relevant authority under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the relevant authority has put the question to the person` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 124(1) the person summoned is legally bound to answer the question truthfully` MEANS
    IF `the person has been summoned by a relevant authority under subsection (1)` AND `the relevant authority has put the question to the person`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the duty is to answer "all questions which the relevant authority may put to the person so summoned"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:4956 | (2) Any person who in any way wilfully obstructs the service of or
-- src:4957 | obedience to the summons, and any person summoned who neglects
-- src:4958 | to attend as required in the summons shall be guilty of an offence.
@ref s 124(2) (src:4956-4958)
GIVEN `the person has in some way wilfully obstructed the service of or obedience to the summons` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person was summoned and has neglected to attend as required in the summons` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 124(2) the person is guilty of an offence` MEANS
    IF `the person has in some way wilfully obstructed the service of or obedience to the summons` OR `the person was summoned and has neglected to attend as required in the summons`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (2) makes guilty the person who wilfully obstructs and the person summoned who neglects to attend; it says nothing of any other conduct
    ELSE NOTHING

-- Section 124(2) states no penalty.  The penalty "for which no penalty is otherwise provided" is s 112's (an extra seam,
-- SEAMS-FOUND.md): the caller passes s 112's answers for a first and a subsequent conviction and says which one this is.
-- src:4956 | (2) Any person who in any way wilfully obstructs the service of or
-- src:4957 | obedience to the summons, and any person summoned who neglects
-- src:4958 | to attend as required in the summons shall be guilty of an offence.
@ref s 124(2) (src:4956-4958)
GIVEN `the answer of s 112 first conviction` IS A MAYBE `Penalty on conviction`
      `the answer of s 112 subsequent conviction` IS A MAYBE `Penalty on conviction`
      `this is a subsequent conviction` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE `Penalty on conviction`
`s 124(2) the penalty for the offence` MEANS
    IF `this is a subsequent conviction`
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): subsection (2) states no penalty; s 112 is the answer, and if it gives none for these facts the Act gives none
    THEN `the answer of s 112 subsequent conviction`
    ELSE `the answer of s 112 first conviction`

-- src:4960 | (3) The Commissioner or inspecting officer has the power to report
-- src:4961 | any failure by such person to attend as required by a summons under
-- src:4962 | subsection (2) to a Magistrate, who may thereupon issue a warrant to
-- src:4963 | secure the attendance of that person as required by the summons.
@ref s 124(3) (src:4960-4963)
GIVEN `the Commissioner or an inspecting officer has reported to a Magistrate the failure of the person to attend as required by the summons` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 124(3) the Magistrate may issue a warrant to secure the attendance of the person` MEANS
    IF `the Commissioner or an inspecting officer has reported to a Magistrate the failure of the person to attend as required by the summons`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the Magistrate's power follows a report by the Commissioner or an inspecting officer
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:4960 | (3) The Commissioner or inspecting officer has the power to report
-- src:4961 | any failure by such person to attend as required by a summons under
-- src:4962 | subsection (2) to a Magistrate, who may thereupon issue a warrant to
-- src:4963 | secure the attendance of that person as required by the summons.
@ref s 124(3) (src:4960-4963)
GIVEN `the person is the Commissioner or an inspecting officer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the failure is a failure to attend as required by a summons under subsection (2)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 124(3) the Commissioner or an inspecting officer has the power to report the failure to a Magistrate` MEANS
    IF `the person is the Commissioner or an inspecting officer` AND `the failure is a failure to attend as required by a summons under subsection (2)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power is of "The Commissioner or inspecting officer" and is to report "any failure by such person to attend as required by a summons under subsection (2)"; the Act does not say another person may not report, or that another failure may not be reported (ruling (c), 2026-11-05)
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 125 Procedure after inquiry`

-- src:4967 | 125.—(1) If upon an inquiry under section 124 it appears that an
-- src:4968 | offence has been committed or that the complaint is well founded, the
-- src:4969 | Commissioner may institute such proceedings, civil or criminal, as
-- src:4970 | the Commissioner considers necessary under the circumstances and
-- src:4971 | where the proceedings arise from a complaint made by an employee
-- src:4972 | or a subcontractor for labour the Commissioner is to institute the
-- src:4973 | proceedings for and in the name of the employee or subcontractor for
-- src:4974 | labour.
@ref s 125(1) (src:4967-4974)
GIVEN `upon an inquiry under section 124 it appears that an offence has been committed or that the complaint is well founded` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 125(1) the Commissioner may institute proceedings, civil or criminal` MEANS
    IF `upon an inquiry under section 124 it appears that an offence has been committed or that the complaint is well founded`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If upon an inquiry under section 124 it appears that an offence has been committed or that the complaint is well founded" (an unmet "if", ruling (b), 2026-11-05)
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:4967 | 125.—(1) If upon an inquiry under section 124 it appears that an
-- src:4968 | offence has been committed or that the complaint is well founded, the
-- src:4969 | Commissioner may institute such proceedings, civil or criminal, as
-- src:4970 | the Commissioner considers necessary under the circumstances and
-- src:4971 | where the proceedings arise from a complaint made by an employee
-- src:4972 | or a subcontractor for labour the Commissioner is to institute the
-- src:4973 | proceedings for and in the name of the employee or subcontractor for
-- src:4974 | labour.
@ref s 125(1) (src:4967-4974)
GIVEN `the answer of s 125(1) the Commissioner may institute proceedings` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the proceedings arise from a complaint made by an employee or a subcontractor for labour` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 125(1) the Commissioner is to institute the proceedings for and in the name of the employee or subcontractor for labour` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "where the proceedings arise from a complaint made by an employee or a subcontractor for labour the Commissioner is to institute the proceedings for and in the name of the employee or subcontractor for labour" (ruling (b), 2026-11-05): proceedings arising from any other complaint are outside it
        IF NOT `the proceedings arise from a complaint made by an employee or a subcontractor for labour` THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If upon an inquiry under section 124 it appears that an offence has been committed or that the complaint is well founded" governs the whole subsection (an unmet "if", ruling (b), 2026-11-05)
        IF `the answer of s 125(1) the Commissioner may institute proceedings` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        IF `the answer of s 125(1) the Commissioner may institute proceedings` EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): whether the Commissioner may institute the proceedings at all is not answered
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- src:4976 | (2) In the event of there being more employees or subcontractors
-- src:4977 | for labour than one making a similar complaint, the Commissioner
-- src:4978 | may, if the Commissioner institutes civil proceedings for and in the
-- src:4979 | name of those employees or subcontractors for labour, consolidate
-- src:4980 | the complaint of all those employees or subcontractors for labour into
-- src:4981 | one cause of action and the Commissioner is only required to take out
-- src:4982 | one summons for and in the name of all those employees or
-- src:4983 | subcontractors for labour in respect of such causes of action.
@ref s 125(2) (src:4976-4983)
GIVEN `more than one employee or subcontractor for labour makes a similar complaint` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Commissioner institutes civil proceedings for and in the name of those employees or subcontractors for labour` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 125(2) the Commissioner may consolidate the complaints into one cause of action and need take out only one summons` MEANS
    IF `more than one employee or subcontractor for labour makes a similar complaint` AND `the Commissioner institutes civil proceedings for and in the name of those employees or subcontractors for labour`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "In the event of there being more employees or subcontractors for labour than one making a similar complaint, the Commissioner may, if the Commissioner institutes civil proceedings ..." ("in the event of" read as "where", assumed not ruled, fork F28; and an unmet "if", ruling (b), 2026-11-05)
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:4985 | (3) Any court which would have jurisdiction to hear and determine
-- src:4986 | separate suits based on such causes of action is competent to hear and
-- src:4987 | determine such consolidated suit, even though the subject matter of
-- src:4988 | the consolidated subject matter suit is in excess of the ordinary
-- src:4989 | jurisdiction of that court.
@ref s 125(3) (src:4985-4989)
GIVEN `the court would have jurisdiction to hear and determine separate suits based on the causes of action` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 125(3) the court is competent to hear and determine the consolidated suit` MEANS
    IF `the court would have jurisdiction to hear and determine separate suits based on the causes of action`
    -- "even though the subject matter of the consolidated suit is in excess of the ordinary jurisdiction of that court"
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the competence is of "any court which would have jurisdiction to hear and determine separate suits"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:4994 | (4) Judgment may be given without any amendment for such one or
-- src:4995 | more of the claimants as may be found to be entitled to relief for such
-- src:4996 | relief as one or more of them may be entitled to.
@ref s 125(4) (src:4994-4996)
GIVEN `the claimant is found to be entitled to relief` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 125(4) judgment may be given without amendment for the claimant found entitled to relief` MEANS
    IF `the claimant is found to be entitled to relief`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the judgment is for "such one or more of the claimants as may be found to be entitled to relief"
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 126 Costs of proceedings`

-- src:5002 | 126. No court fees are chargeable in the first instance on any
-- src:5003 | proceedings commenced by an employee or a subcontractor for
-- src:5004 | labour or by the Commissioner on behalf of the employee or the
-- src:5005 | subcontractor for labour, against the employee’s employer or other
-- src:5006 | person liable under this Act but, in case a conviction is had or
@ref s 126 (src:5002-5006)
GIVEN `the proceedings were commenced by an employee or a subcontractor for labour, or by the Commissioner on behalf of the employee or the subcontractor for labour, against the employee's employer or other person liable under this Act` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 126 court fees are chargeable in the first instance on the proceedings` MEANS
    IF `the proceedings were commenced by an employee or a subcontractor for labour, or by the Commissioner on behalf of the employee or the subcontractor for labour, against the employee's employer or other person liable under this Act`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "No court fees are chargeable in the first instance on any proceedings commenced by an employee or a subcontractor for labour"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the rule is for proceedings commenced by an employee, a subcontractor for labour or the Commissioner on their behalf against the employer or other person liable
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:5006 | person liable under this Act but, in case a conviction is had or
-- src:5007 | judgment is given against that employer or person liable, the court
-- src:5008 | fees are to be paid by the employer or person liable together with the
-- src:5009 | general costs of the proceedings.
@ref s 126 (src:5006-5009)
GIVEN `a conviction is had or judgment is given against the employer or person liable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 126 the employer or person liable is to pay the court fees together with the general costs` MEANS
    IF `a conviction is had or judgment is given against the employer or person liable`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the fees are to be paid "in case a conviction is had or judgment is given against that employer or person liable"; the Act does not say who pays them otherwise
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Part 15 -- the duties and permissions as regulative rules`

-- Each rule below says who must, may or shant do what, and by when, in the order of the section.  Each has a decision
-- function above that answers the same question over the facts of one case; the decision functions are the ones
-- the tests assert, because a `#TRACE` result cannot be asserted on the main binary (NOTES.md, "What the main binary does not accept").

DECLARE `Part 15 Party` IS ONE OF
    `s 115(3C) the party absent`
    `s 117(1) the person interested`
    `s 119(1)(b) the Commissioner`
    `s 120 a party before the Commissioner`
    `s 124(1) the person summoned`

DECLARE `Part 15 Act` IS ONE OF
    `s 115(3C) apply to set aside or vary the order`
    `s 117(1) appeal to the General Division of the High Court`
    `s 119(1)(b) summon in writing the party against whom the claim is made`
    `s 120 be represented by an advocate or solicitor or a paid agent`
    `s 124(1) attend at the time and place specified in the summons`

-- src:4677 | (3B) Subject to subsection (3C), any order made by the
-- src:4678 | Commissioner under subsection (1) in the absence of a party
-- src:4679 | concerned or affected by the order may be set aside or varied by
-- src:4680 | the Commissioner, on the application of that party, on such terms as
-- src:4681 | the Commissioner thinks just.
-- src:4685 | (3C) An application to set aside or vary an order made by the
-- src:4686 | Commissioner referred to in subsection (3B) must be made no later
-- src:4687 | than 14 days after the date of the order.
@ref s 115(3B) and (3C) (src:4677-4687)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Part 15 Party` `Part 15 Act`
`s 115(3C) rule` MEANS
    PARTY `s 115(3C) the party absent`
    MAY `s 115(3C) apply to set aside or vary the order`
    WITHIN 14
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST FULFILLED

-- src:4739 | 117.—(1) Where any person interested is dissatisfied with the
-- src:4740 | decision or order of the Commissioner, the person may, within
-- src:4741 | 14 days after the decision or order, appeal to the General Division of
-- src:4742 | the High Court from the decision or order.
@ref s 117(1) (src:4739-4742)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Part 15 Party` `Part 15 Act`
`s 117(1) rule` MEANS
    PARTY `s 117(1) the person interested`
    MAY `s 117(1) appeal to the General Division of the High Court`
    WITHIN 14
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST FULFILLED

-- src:4785 | (b) upon receipt of the memorandum or verbal claim and of the
-- src:4786 | registration fee payable by the person in accordance with
-- src:4787 | the rates specified in the Second Schedule, the
-- src:4788 | Commissioner must summon in writing the party against
-- src:4789 | whom the claim is made, giving reasonable notice to that
-- src:4790 | party of the nature of the claim and the time and place at
-- src:4791 | which the claim will be inquired into, and the
-- src:4792 | Commissioner must also notify or summon all persons
-- src:4793 | whose interests appear to the Commissioner likely to be
-- src:4794 | affected by the proceedings;
@ref s 119(1)(b) (src:4785-4794)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Part 15 Party` `Part 15 Act`
`s 119(1)(b) rule` MEANS
    PARTY `s 119(1)(b) the Commissioner`
    MUST `s 119(1)(b) summon in writing the party against whom the claim is made`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH

-- Derived from the flat negative (N2): the party shant be represented by an advocate or solicitor or a paid agent.
-- src:4873 | 120. In proceedings before the Commissioner, a party —
-- src:4875 | (a) being an employee and a member of a trade union that has
-- src:4876 | been given recognition under Part 3 of the Industrial
-- src:4877 | Relations Act 1960 by the employer of the employee, may
-- src:4878 | be represented by an officer of the trade union; and
-- src:4880 | (b) being an employer may be represented by one of the
-- src:4881 | employer’s employees,
-- src:4883 | but must not be represented by an advocate or solicitor or a paid
-- src:4884 | agent.
@ref s 120 (src:4873-4884)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Part 15 Party` `Part 15 Act`
`s 120 rule` MEANS
    PARTY `s 120 a party before the Commissioner`
    SHANT `s 120 be represented by an advocate or solicitor or a paid agent`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH

-- src:4944 | the relevant authority may summon any other person who the relevant
-- src:4945 | authority has reason to believe can give information regarding the
-- src:4950 | subject matter of the inquiry or complaint, and the person so
-- src:4951 | summoned is legally bound to attend at the time and place specified in
-- src:4952 | the summons to furnish information or documents, produce any
-- src:4953 | article or give statements, and to answer truthfully all questions which
-- src:4954 | the relevant authority may put to the person so summoned.
@ref s 124(1) (src:4944-4954)
GIVEN days IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Part 15 Party` `Part 15 Act`
`s 124(1) rule` MEANS
    PARTY `s 124(1) the person summoned`
    MUST `s 124(1) attend at the time and place specified in the summons`
    WITHIN days
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH

#TRACE `s 115(3C) rule` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `s 115(3C) the party absent` DOES `s 115(3C) apply to set aside or vary the order` AT 14

#TRACE `s 115(3C) rule` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `s 115(3C) the party absent` DOES `s 115(3C) apply to set aside or vary the order` AT 15

